{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795714490,"nonce":"86UYCBaz0s/cvy8h1q0vJZWDDus=","title":"precond.form(): the default inner solve has no cuDSS route on a GPU","message":"**What goes wrong.** `jno.precond.form([...])` with no `inner=` factors the assembled auxiliary operator once and applies the factors on every Krylov iteration. The factorization is on the host (MKL PARDISO where installed, SuperLU otherwise) behind a `jax.pure_callback`, whatever the solve's device. On a GPU in float64 with cuDSS installed, `jno.solve.lu()` would factor on the device; the preconditioner never asks. So a GPU solve pays a host factorization, a host-to-device copy of the vector per iteration, and keeps the auxiliary on the CPU.\n\n**Reproduce.** On a GPU in float64 with cuDSS installed, solve a periodic N1E Maxwell cell with `linear=jno.solve.gmres(...)` and `precond=jno.precond.form([curlcurl - k^2 eps mass, local faces])`, with no `inner=`. Every application calls the host factors. Read from `_Form.materialize` in jno/precond.py (`FactorOnce`, `pure_callback`); not timed. #240 asks for the same backend as `lu()` as a direction; the memory half (PARDISO instead of SuperLU on the 2n block) is done on the branch named in the label, the GPU half is not.\n\n**Expected.** With a GPU solve and cuDSS available, the default inner factors on the device once and applies the factors inside the compiled solve, as `lu(backend=\"cudss\")` does, so no callback sits in the iteration. Where cuDSS is not available the host factors stay the default.\n\n**Where.** jno/precond.py `_Form.materialize` (the `self.inner is None` branch); jno/solve.py `_preferred_direct_backend`; the cuDSS plan and factor-slot code in jno/utils/solver/linear.py.\n\n**Workaround.** None for the callback; to avoid the host factorization, pass an iterative inner (`inner=jno.solve.cg(...)`) or solve directly with `lu(backend=\"cudss\")`.\n\n**Direction.** Add a cuDSS-backed factor-once applier next to `FactorOnce`, with the same `solve` and `solve_t` contract, chosen by the same rule as `lu()`. Measure one GPU Krylov solve against the host callback at a few sizes before keeping it: the callback may win for a small auxiliary.","files":null}]}